---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";
export function getStaticPaths(){return projects.map((project)=>({params:{slug:project.slug},props:{project}}));}
const {project}=Astro.props;
const injaz=project.slug==="injaz";
const life=project.slug==="life-advise";
const hotel=project.slug==="hotel-room-rental";
const chat=project.slug==="chat-room";
---
<SiteLayout title={`${project.name} — Osama Ibrahim`} description={project.description}>
<section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><a class="read-link" href="/projects/">← All projects</a><small class="eyebrow"> / {project.category}</small><h1>{project.name}</h1><p>{project.description}</p><div class="case-meta"><span>{project.status}</span>{project.stack.map((item)=><span>{item}</span>)}</div><img class="case-image" src={project.image} alt={`${project.name} project screenshot`}/></div></section>
<section class="section"><article class="container simple-content">
{injaz ? <><small class="eyebrow">PRODUCT CASE STUDY / IN PROGRESS</small><h2>Bringing organizational work into one clear system.</h2><p>INJAZ is an organizational management platform being developed to help teams structure everyday work, responsibilities and operational records. Its product direction is built around clarity, accountable workflows and practical administration.</p><h3>What is being built</h3><p>Current product work includes role-aware access, employee and department administration, operational reporting and claims workflows. The platform remains in development; this page does not claim a public production release.</p><h3>Engineering focus</h3><p>The implementation uses a web application architecture with Supabase and PostgreSQL at its data layer. Requirements, access boundaries and end-to-end scenarios are being validated before release.</p><div class="evidence"><b>Evidence status</b><p>In development. A public demo will be added only after a verified deployment is approved for public viewing.</p></div></>
: life ? <><small class="eyebrow">PROJECT CASE STUDY / RECOVERY</small><h2>An offline-first experience for thoughtful advice.</h2><p>Life-Advise was created as a quotes and advice application designed to remain useful when connectivity is limited. Its source repository confirms a React application using Dexie for local data storage.</p><h3>Current state</h3><p>This is historical work currently being reviewed for recovery. The previously listed public URL returned a Cloudflare 522 response when checked on 13 November 2026. The source repository remains available as a historical code reference.</p><div class="evidence"><b>Evidence status</b><p>Historical project · recovery in progress. The former demo is currently unreachable; no live-demo claim is made.</p></div></>
: hotel ? <><small class="eyebrow">PROJECT CASE STUDY / HISTORICAL</small><h2>A room discovery concept for travellers.</h2><p>{project.description}</p><h3>Current evidence</h3><p>The linked source repository identifies a React application using Contentful. The previously listed public URL currently presents a Beach Resort page rather than the room-rental project, so it has been removed as a project demo link.</p><div class="evidence"><b>Evidence status</b><p>Historical project. Source repository retained; previous deployment destination did not match this project when checked.</p></div></>
: chat ? <><small class="eyebrow">PROJECT CASE STUDY / HISTORICAL WORK</small><h2>A chat interface built around rooms and conversations.</h2><p>{project.description}</p><h3>What the source confirms</h3><p>The linked repository is the client application and its package manifest confirms React. The original portfolio also mentioned Node.js and Socket.io, but those server-side technologies are not verified by this client repository and are not presented as confirmed stack items.</p><div class="evidence"><b>Evidence status</b><p>Historical client repository. Server-side implementation remains unverified.</p></div></>
: <><small class="eyebrow">PROJECT RECORD / HISTORICAL WORK</small><h2>{project.description}</h2><h3>What the archive establishes</h3><p>The original portfolio records this project and the technology notes shown above. Where a source repository is linked, its existence is preserved as a code reference; that alone does not establish that the original application still runs or that the historical deployment remains available.</p><h3>What is not reconstructed</h3><p>The surviving public record does not provide enough detail to reliably recreate the original requirements, implementation trade-offs, or lessons learned. Rather than invent a project narrative, this entry stays with the evidence that remains.</p><div class="evidence"><b>Evidence status</b><p>Historical project. Current deployment and runtime compatibility are unverified.</p></div></>}
<div class="case-links">{project.source && <a class="read-link" href={project.source} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Source repository ↗</a>}{project.reference && <a class="read-link" href={project.reference} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Previously listed link ↗</a>}{project.live && <a class="read-link" href={project.live} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Previously listed URL ↗</a>}</div>
</article></section>
</SiteLayout>
